'''Andrea Jana Navarro-Palacios''' (born 9 September 1972 in [[Rosario]], [[Cerdanya]]) is a [[Balisca|Baliscan]] politician who served [[Secretary General of Balisca]] from 4 January 2019 to 27 July 2021. A member of the [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]], she became the third and last premier to come from the [[W:platformism|platform]] before it's dissolution in 2022. 

After graduating from the [[University of Cerdanya, La Plata]] with a [[W:Bachelor of Education|degree in education]] she worked as a [[W:Secondary school|secondary school]] teacher. Turning to elective politics, Palacios was elected in the [[2009 Baliscan confederal election]] to represent the [[Figueres]] [[commune district]]. She served as the [[Secretariat of Education and Integration (Balisca)|Secretary of Education and Integration]] from 2012 to 2019 under [[Cristiano Sandoval]]. Palacios entered the [[2018 Baliscan premiership election]], ideologically aligning herself with the [[W:New Left|New Left]]. Despite not being favored in almost all forecasts, Palacios defeated six other candidates in the premiership race. Her election was largely viewed as an [[W:Upset (competition)|electoral upset]] against the [[W:Left-wing nationalism|left-wing nationalist]] rhetoric of her primary opponent [[Josuè Subirà]]. Palacios was elected by a margin of 62,784 votes (0.08%) in the final round, the narrowest result in Baliscan history. 

Palacios' tenure was undermined by congressional setbacks and gridlock, and by the end of her first year in office her approval rating had fallen to 33%. Her reformist oriented program caused significant fighting between her liberal Reformist Bloc and it's majority {{W|anti-establishment}} partners in the [[United Left]], resulting in significant portions of her program never being enacted. She held office through the first waves of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} and the resulting [[W:COVID-19 recession|COVID-19 recession]] starting in 2020, during which her proactive response gathered widespread praise. 

Palacios initially avoided becoming embroiled in the crisis surrounding the [[West Pacific]]'s detention of the [[Baliscan duo]], instead delegating the task to Deputy [[Zavier Bilal]]. This ultimately resulted in his invocation of [[Basic Law: Universal Humanism]] (Art. 10) against the government of the West Pacific. This response to the crisis was widely praised by the Baliscan public, leading to significant rises in government approval ratings. Bilal and several other secretaries later broke with Palacios following her personal intervention to accept the proposed Macquarie Agreement between Baliscan and Pasifikan negotiators regarding the fate of the Baliscan duo. After an online petition posted in June had attracted nearly 4 million signatures calling for her resignation, mass demonstrations began on 11 July. Imminently facing a [[W:Recall vote|recall vote]] and a [[W:Vote of no confidence|loss of congressional confidence]], Palacios made a televised address on 27 July announcing her resignation from political office.

